Vendored Wayland protocol bindings
==================================

This directory holds Wayland protocol definitions and the client-side C bindings generated from
them. Both the XML and the generated code are checked in.


Why vendor the generated code?
------------------------------

Generating these at build time would make DGL's Wayland backend depend on two extra things being
installed on every build machine:

  * the `wayland-protocols` data package, which ships the XML
  * the `wayland-scanner` binary, which turns XML into C

Neither is needed at run time, neither is needed to *link*, and neither is universally present --
`wayland-protocols` in particular is a build-time-only package that plenty of otherwise complete
Wayland systems (and most cross-compilation sysroots) do not have. The generated code is small,
stable, and licensed permissively, so checking it in removes both dependencies: a machine with the
Wayland *client libraries* can build the backend, full stop.

This is the same reasoning behind `dgl/src/khronos` for the MSVC GL headers.

Makefile.base.mk still probes for the tooling and reports it as `HAVE_WAYLAND_TOOLS` in
`make features`, but only as information for whoever wants to regenerate this directory. It gates
nothing; `HAVE_WAYLAND` depends on the libraries alone.


Origin
------

Fetched 2026-07-26 from the `main` branch of

    https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/wayland/wayland-protocols

at commit afb614d5fcbd02d261a6ae91920aa91cf3915a8a
("stable/linux-dmabuf: Spell out rules around format_table events", 2026-06-23).

  XML file                          upstream path
  --------------------------------  ---------------------------------------------------------
  xdg-shell.xml                     stable/xdg-shell/xdg-shell.xml
  viewporter.xml                    stable/viewporter/viewporter.xml
  fractional-scale-v1.xml           staging/fractional-scale/fractional-scale-v1.xml
  xdg-decoration-unstable-v1.xml    unstable/xdg-decoration/xdg-decoration-unstable-v1.xml

Interface versions in the generated code:

  xdg_wm_base, xdg_positioner, xdg_surface, xdg_toplevel, xdg_popup   7
  wp_viewporter, wp_viewport                                          1
  wp_fractional_scale_manager_v1, wp_fractional_scale_v1              1
  zxdg_decoration_manager_v1, zxdg_toplevel_decoration_v1             2

Note that these are the versions the *bindings* can speak. wayland.c binds each global at
`MIN(advertised, what this backend actually implements)`, so a newer protocol version here never
makes the backend demand more of a compositor than before.

Each protocol XML carries its own MIT/X11-style licence notice, which the generator copies into both
the header and the source. Nothing here is under DPF's ISC licence.


Regenerating
------------

With wayland-scanner installed, from this directory:

    for p in xdg-shell viewporter fractional-scale-v1 xdg-decoration-unstable-v1; do
        wayland-scanner client-header "$p.xml" "$p-client-protocol.h"
        wayland-scanner private-code   "$p.xml" "$p-protocol.c"
    done

To also refresh the XML, download it from the upstream paths in the table above and update the
commit and date recorded here.

`private-code` (rather than `public-code`) is deliberate: it marks the generated interface objects
`WL_PRIVATE`, i.e. hidden visibility, so a DPF plugin cannot collide with a host that has linked its
own copy of the same protocol.


How this is compiled
--------------------

dgl/src/pugl.cpp includes all four `-protocol.c` files at global scope inside `extern "C"`, before
the matching `-client-protocol.h` headers and before it enters the DGL namespace. The ordering and
the reasons for it are explained in the comment there; in short, the marshalling code has to keep C
linkage and has to be the first declaration the compiler sees for its own symbols.

There is no separate compilation step and no entry in any Makefile or CMakeLists: these files are
part of the pugl.cpp unity build.
